---
/**
 * Terms and Conditions. Draft. Needs a human read before launch.
 *
 * The entity paragraph adapts to whether business.legalName is still a
 * placeholder. Right now no entity is filed, so the page says so plainly
 * rather than naming a company that does not exist. The moment an LLC is
 * registered, filling in legalName in src/config/business.ts switches this
 * page to the corporate wording.
 */
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { business, real } from "../config/business";
import { plans } from "../content/pricing";

const legalName = real(business.legalName);
const [website, retainer] = plans;
---

<Legal
  title={`Terms and conditions: ${business.name}`}
  description="What each service covers, when payment is due, how revisions work, what we need from you, who owns the finished work, and the limits of our liability."
  path="/terms"
  heading="Terms and conditions"
  standfirst="These are the terms you agree to when you hire us. We have tried to write them in plain English rather than in the usual language."
>
  <h2>Who you are contracting with</h2>
  {
    legalName ? (
      <p>
        {business.name} is a trade name of {legalName}, registered in the state
        of {business.state}. {legalName} is the party you are contracting with,
        and these terms are governed by {business.state} law.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        {business.name} is the trading name of a partnership between Zain
        Bahalim, Roshan Mohammad and Abayjit Singh, based in {business.city},
        {" "}{business.state}. We have not yet registered a company. That means
        you are contracting with the three of us personally, and these terms
        are governed by {business.state} law. When we do register an entity,
        this page will name it and we will tell existing clients in writing.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2>What each service covers</h2>

  <h3>{website.name}, {website.amount} {website.unit}</h3>
  <p>{website.summary} The work covers:</p>
  <ul>
    {website.includes?.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p><strong>How long.</strong> {website.timeline}</p>
  <p><strong>Not included.</strong> {website.excludes}</p>

  <h3>{retainer.name}, {retainer.amount} {retainer.unit}</h3>
  <p>{retainer.summary}</p>
  {
    retainer.parts?.map((part) => (
      <div>
        <p><strong>{part.name}.</strong> {part.summary}</p>
        <ul>
          {part.includes.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p><strong>How long.</strong> {part.timeline}</p>
      </div>
    ))
  }
  <p><strong>Not included.</strong> {retainer.excludes}</p>
  <p>
    Taking both services does not change the price of either. There is no
    bundle rate and no setup fee.
  </p>

  <h2>Payment</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The website.</strong> {website.amount} in total, split in two.
      Half is due before we start and half is due on the day the site goes
      live. We invoice by email and payment is due within seven days of the
      invoice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The monthly retainer.</strong> {retainer.amount} a month, charged
      in advance on the same date each month. The first month is charged on the
      day we start.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do not charge interest on late payment. If an invoice goes more than
    thirty days past due we will stop work and tell you why, and we will not
    hand over logins for unpaid work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Prices are in US dollars and do not include any sales tax that may apply.
  </p>

  <h2>Revisions</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>On a website.</strong> Two rounds of changes are included before
    the site goes live. A round means you send us one list of everything you
    want changed and we work through it. Sending three items on Monday and two
    more on Wednesday counts as two rounds, so it is worth collecting them up.
  </p>
  <p>
    After the site is live, tell us what you need and we will quote it in
    writing before we do anything. If it is a small text change we will show
    you how to do it yourself on a call at no charge.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>On content and ads.</strong> Nothing is published or handed over
    until you have approved it. Ask for as many changes as you like on each
    piece. What is fixed is the volume, which is eight posts a month and one
    round of new ad creative a month, not the number of edits.
  </p>

  <h2>What we need from you</h2>
  <p>
    Almost everything we are late on is something we are waiting for. You are
    responsible for supplying:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Accurate details about the business: hours, service area, what you charge
      if you want it listed, and the licences or registrations you want shown.
    </li>
    <li>
      Photographs you own or have permission to use. If you send us a picture,
      you are telling us you have the right to publish it. We will not use
      images pulled from a search engine and we will not generate fake ones.
    </li>
    <li>
      Access to your domain and any existing accounts we need, or the authority
      to register them in your name.
    </li>
    <li>Answers within a reasonable time when we ask a question.</li>
    <li>
      Approval of anything published under your name, including posts and ad
      copy. You have the final read.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If we cannot get what we need for more than sixty days, we may close the
    job and invoice for the work done up to that point.
  </p>

  <h2>Who owns the finished work</h2>
  <p>
    You do, once you have paid in full. That covers the website, its text, its
    layout, the posts we write for you and the ad creative we build. On the day
    the site goes live and the balance is paid, the domain is in your name, the
    hosting account is in your name, and every login is yours.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things stay ours. The first is the general know how and technique we
    use, which we obviously go on using for other clients. The second is any
    open source component in the site, which stays under its own licence, and
    those licences are recorded in the project.
  </p>
  <p>
    We would like to show your site in our own portfolio and mention that we
    built it. If you would rather we did not, say so and we will not.
  </p>

  <h2>Cancelling</h2>
  <p>
    Refunds and notice periods are set out in full on the
    {" "}<a href="/refunds">refund policy</a> page. In short: the deposit on a
    website covers work already begun and is not refundable, the balance is not
    charged if you cancel before launch, and the monthly retainer needs
    fourteen days notice.
  </p>

  <h2>What we promise, and what we do not</h2>
  <p>
    We promise the work will be done with reasonable care and skill, that the
    site will work on a current phone and desktop browser, and that we will fix
    anything broken that is our fault, free, for thirty days after launch.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not promise business results. Nobody honestly can. We cannot promise
    a particular position in Google, a number of phone calls, a cost per lead,
    or that any given ad will work. If somebody in this industry offers you a
    guaranteed ranking, that is a good reason to walk away from them.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not control the platforms. Google, Meta, your host and your domain
    registrar all change their rules and their prices without asking us, and
    any of them can suspend an account for reasons they do not explain. We will
    help you deal with it, but it is not something we can be held to.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits on liability</h2>
  <p>
    If we get something wrong and it costs you money, our total liability is
    capped at what you paid us for the work in question. For a website that is
    {" "}{website.amount}. For the retainer it is the fees you paid us in the
    three months before the problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    We are not liable for lost profit, lost business, lost data, or costs you
    incur with a third party, and neither of us is liable for a failure caused
    by something genuinely outside our control.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing in these terms limits any liability that cannot be limited under
    {" "}{business.state} law, including under the Texas Deceptive Trade
    Practices Act.
  </p>

  <h2>Disagreements</h2>
  <p>
    Email us first. Almost everything is a misunderstanding that one phone call
    fixes, and we would rather fix it than argue about it. If that fails, these
    terms are governed by the law of the state of {business.state} and any
    dispute goes to the courts of Harris County, Texas.
  </p>

  <h2>If these terms change</h2>
  <p>
    The date at the top changes with them. The terms that apply to your job are
    the ones in force on the day we agreed the work, and we will not change
    them mid job without telling you.
  </p>
</Legal>
